A||||6||K9||One tie-rod or all tie-rods?||||11
Z||000000||K9||11-02-1999||10:50 AM||||I need to replace one (can"t tell exactly which one) of my inner tie-rod end but can't tell if there is any other worn connections in by front end. My question is, if I should replace all the rods.( inner and outer at each side)or mabythe rack too. Also what would be the cost (Can. doller)for parts and labour?||||reg||11
Z||000001||theogre||11-02-1999||12:34 PM||||I believe the replacement racks usually come with the inners attached.<P>the outers are easy to check. the inners are a pain. the hardest part is keeping the steering wheel in one place. you also need to take the boot clamps off to make sure if the rod end or the rack is where the play is.<P>if you have to check the thing by yourself try lashing a 2x4 to the steering wheel like a big "club" then set a cinder block or something heavy on the end of the board.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||11-02-1999||08:48 PM||||I've replace my tie rod before, on the driverside.  I done mine the hardway, but if you go to pepboys, or one of those large automotive parts store, there is a large socket that fits over the nut on the inner tie rod.  This way is easier then using a wrench, which is impossible, because of the lack of room.  Go with the socket and there is some special tool to put the boot back on, i still dont have it and a zip tie is holding mine temp.  Its not a hard job if you got the tools.  I got my inner tie rod from the fiero store and took less then a week for me to get it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ozzy||11-02-1999||11:53 PM||||I thought I had a shot inner and it turned out to be play in the passenger side steering rack bushing. Stupid thing is made outta plastic. Replacement rack: '88 Coupe,<BR>$230.00. (Rebuilt with Brass bushings and grease nimples)||||reg||1
Z||000004||K9||11-03-1999||10:37 AM||||OZZY, What is this play in the drivers side rack that you are talking about. How do I check for it and is it fixible? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-03-1999||03:02 PM||||someone makes a replacement for that bushing that is available as a single part if that's all that's wrong. I forget who makes it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||11-03-1999||07:37 PM||||Inner tie rods do commonly wear out on high mileage Fieros. I've had to replace them on two of my cars. US prices for Moog brand tie rods are about $50 each for the parts (inner or outer) and about $50 each for labor. Although a bit of a chore, they can be replaced by a shade-tree, weekend mechanic. The car should be taken to a shop afterwards and given an alignment.||||reg||1
